Subject: A draw's table is not zeroed whole; drawbench records without submitting, and in passes

Every reused slab was zeroed when a command buffer took it: 256 KiB for every 32 draws' tables, 8 KiB a draw
(CommandBuffer::piece under libsystem_platform in perfil-1: about 1 us a draw on the phone; 8% of a recorded draw on
the host). Of its table a draw's GPU work reads what the draw writes: the descriptors its loads name, as many as
they name; the copies of constants the stream itself makes there; and the words the stream addresses singly, all
below the border colours (a fetch's flush, an occlusion query's two snapshots).

- CommandQueue::takeSlab hands a slab back as it is and says so; CommandBuffer::piece zeroes each piece as it is
  cut from such a slab, as before for every caller but one.
- CommandBuffer::draw takes its table (and the second one of a pass recorded both ways) as is and zeroes its first
  128 bytes.
- CommandQueue::poisonReused, for the tests and the golden streams: a slab that comes back is filled with 0xa5, so
  that what a draw leaves unwritten is not last frame's tables for the same draw. The golden sums of what the loads
  name are what they were.

tools/drawbench.cpp:
- --record N: a C scene's frame recorded N more times and let go unsubmitted, so that a sampling profiler sees a
  process that records draws and little else.
- scene U: T's frame in five passes of 22 draws, as the phone's command buffers hold it (4.9 passes and 128 to 140
  draws each): what a pass costs beside its draws.
- the vertex stage's textures are told once a frame, as the plug-in tells them from now on (at an encoder's first
  draw and when they change), not before every draw.


